<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2025-01-24.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="000037"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#000037" label="[00:00:37]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: would you mind to also review <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/oriansj/M2-Planet/pull/65/">https://github.com/oriansj/M2-Planet/pull/65/</a>  from gtker?</span></div><div class="line" id="003306"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#003306" label="[00:33:06]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: on it</span></div><div class="line" id="003321"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#003321" label="[00:33:21]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">thanks. I need to take another look at it too</span></div><div class="line" id="003331"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#003331" label="[00:33:31]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but in general looks useful</span></div><div class="line" id="003333"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#003333" label="[00:33:33]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Ooh, this is a feature I want</span></div><div class="line" id="003337"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#003337" label="[00:33:37]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah...</span></div><div class="line" id="003539"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#003539" label="[00:35:39]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">structs are getting much better</span></div><div class="line" id="003601"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#003601" label="[00:36:01]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">couple of years ago only struct pointers allocated with calloc were supported</span></div><div class="line" id="003722"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#003722" label="[00:37:22]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this *looks* fine from an overview apart from style things you've pointed out</span></div><div class="line" id="004125"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#004125" label="[00:41:25]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">pointer arithmetic is probably the feature I miss the most from M2-Planet now...</span></div><div class="line" id="004151"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#004151" label="[00:41:51]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I guess cause it's quite confusing to everybody new to the code</span></div><div class="line" id="004458"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#004458" label="[00:44:58]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Clearly my best hope for escaping from c, are people really good at c.</span></div><div class="line" id="004647"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#004647" label="[00:46:47]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">M2-Planet builds -fast-. If it could eventually replace Mes that would be amazing. Mes solves an important problem but it is very very slow and complex.</span></div><div class="line" id="005507"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#005507" label="[00:55:07]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, if compilers are good enough, there could be more than one path for bootstrapping</span></div><div class="line" id="005847"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#005847" label="[00:58:47]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">True. Multiple independent paths getting the same result can help build confidence in code that is otherwise hard to review or reason about.</span></div><div class="line" id="010005"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#010005" label="[01:00:05]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Exactly why I am pivoting to go even before the first gcc is built so we can have any higher level tooling in go, a -very- independent path.</span></div><div class="line" id="010038"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#010038" label="[01:00:38]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but the path to tinycc is still the most sketchy bit</span></div><div class="line" id="010222"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#010222" label="[01:02:22]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">If that other path ZilchOS took to go from tinycc to musl without Mes is vaible, then we are all set for building go even before Mes</span></div><div class="line" id="010257"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#010257" label="[01:02:57]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">then maybe implement autogen replacement in go or something insane. tbd!</span></div><div class="line" id="010517"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#010517" label="[01:05:17]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, hrm, they -start- with tinycc so no getting around mes just yet. Still. Can pivot immediately after mes at least</span></div><div class="line" id="011359"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#011359" label="[01:13:59]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, tcc is generally after mes</span></div><div class="line" id="011415"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#011415" label="[01:14:15]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so I don't think you'll actually win that much by following zilchOS path</span></div><div class="line" id="011445"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#011445" label="[01:14:45]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but up to you</span></div><div class="line" id="013807"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#013807" label="[01:38:07]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Well if nothing else early busybox lets me drop a -lot- of deps, so might as well take that easier win for a first pass</span></div><div class="line" id="014039"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#014039" label="[01:40:39]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'll probably understand a lot more non obvious blockers as  Idive in</span></div><div class="line" id="014641"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#014641" label="[01:46:41]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we don't use mes after we build tinycc in live-bootstrap either....</span></div><div class="line" id="014650"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#014650" label="[01:46:50]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we still use meslibc for a little bit though</span></div><div class="line" id="014656"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#014656" label="[01:46:56]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but that's fairly inconsequential</span></div><div class="line" id="014717"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#014717" label="[01:47:17]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yeah that is why I corrected myself. the ZilchOS path only saves me steps between mes and gcc4+</span></div><div class="line" id="014746"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#014746" label="[01:47:46]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and for now I will try stikonas suggestion to speed up Mes with concatinating all hhe c files</span></div><div class="line" id="014907"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014907" label="[01:49:07]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh and fossy wrote some tool for things we try to avoid in live-bootstrap <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/fosslinux/problematic-source">https://github.com/fosslinux/problematic-source</a>  (though we haven't checked the whole live-bootstrap with it)</span></div><div class="line" id="042445"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#042445" label="[04:24:45]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: Guix currently builds GHC 4.08.2 from .hc quasisource files, and then bootstraps up to GHC 7.0 (pending bugfix for glibc regression that breaks gcc 4.9, needed by ghc 6.6)</span></div><div class="line" id="042531"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#042531" label="[04:25:31]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">From then on, I have a patch that goes from 7.0 to 7.6, and then to 7.10, on x86-32 only</span></div><div class="line" id="042615"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#042615" label="[04:26:15]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Then, Guix has a full and proper bootstrap path from 7.10 to 9.2 and 9.4, so long as no 32-&gt;64-bit changeover is needed</span></div><div class="line" id="042708"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#042708" label="[04:27:08]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Putting all of that together, we can go from the 4.08.2 .hc quasisources (C code generated by an earlier GHC from Haskell source code) all the way up to 9.x on 32-bit x86</span></div><div class="line" id="042832"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#042832" label="[04:28:32]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">On x86-64, the process is currently stuck on needing a cross compilation step - I tried doing that with 7.10 (which, per GHC wiki, is one of the first versions to officially support cross compilation), but ran into problems - I'm hoping 8.x can perform this cross compilation step.</span></div><div class="line" id="042921"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#042921" label="[04:29:21]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(Guix's main branch currently builds 7.10 using prebuilt bootstrap binaries of 7.8; my patch eliminates that on 32-bit x86.)</span></div><div class="line" id="043013"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#043013" label="[04:30:13]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">So, if we can build GHC 4.08.2 using MicroHs on 32-bit x86, we can complete the bootstrap chain on that platform.</span></div><div class="line" id="073135"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#073135" label="[07:31:35]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh nice, so we have a chain from 4.08.2 -&gt; 9.4</span></div><div class="line" id="073147"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#073147" label="[07:31:47]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's pretty sick</span></div><div class="line" id="073156"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#073156" label="[07:31:56]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(good)</span></div><div class="line" id="074132"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#074132" label="[07:41:32]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator keep in mind if compiling ghc with ghc takes 5 hours, compiling it with microhs takes 355 hours (24 days 19 hours), this is how I calculated: (20 stage0 + 50 stage1 + 1 stage2) * 5</span></div><div class="line" id="074230"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#074230" label="[07:42:30]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">where stage0 is microhs being 20 times slower than ghc, stage1 is where binary produced by mhs is 50 times slower than it would be by ghc, stage2 is just too ensure reproducible builds, that stage2 and stage1 are bit-by-bit identical</span></div><div class="line" id="074342"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#074342" label="[07:43:42]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also how is arm and riscv support in ghc 4.08.2?</span></div><div class="line" id="074518"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#074518" label="[07:45:18]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">like if you take 4.08.2 ghc and microhs, how do you realistically make bootstrap on arm and riscv? throw x86 emulator and turn 355 hours into thousands of hours?</span></div><div class="line" id="074620"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#074620" label="[07:46:20]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">solution: never use ghc, pick haskell projects you need and compile them with microhs</span></div><div class="line" id="075116"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#075116" label="[07:51:16]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also nhc98 is still packaged in guix, but is not bootstrap, this is where it's more realistic to use microhs to bootstrap another haskell compiler</span></div><div class="line" id="082319"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#082319" label="[08:23:19]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">where did 20 &amp; 50 come from</span></div><div class="line" id="083158"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#083158" label="[08:31:58]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy from Lennart's presentation on youtube where somewhere in the middle of video he tells &quot;why not combinators?&quot; and there he measured microhs and ghc performance, I took those numbers and performed basic division to get 20 and 50</span></div><div class="line" id="083255"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#083255" label="[08:32:55]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">can nhc98 bootstrap off either hugs or mhs?</span></div><div class="line" id="083319"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#083319" label="[08:33:19]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">gotcha.</span></div><div class="line" id="083521"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#083521" label="[08:35:21]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy it might be this video <a rel="nofollow" href="https://redirect.invidious.io/watch?v=uMurx1a6Zck">https://redirect.invidious.io/watch?v=uMurx1a6Zck</a>  but I can't double-check my memory because google has went into hostile mode so much I can't even use yt-dlp</span></div><div class="line" id="083750"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#083750" label="[08:37:50]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">jackdk rekado tried and failed to do it with hugs, but nobody tried with microhs, and it is pointless now if you are not willing to extend nhc98, because: 1. nhc98 is less portable than hugs and microhs; 2. microhs is the biggest language after ghc</span></div><div class="line" id="084325"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#084325" label="[08:43:25]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in its current state nhc98 is a toy compiler that likely produces faster binaries than microhs, but available on very few platforms and supports very few extentions</span></div><div class="line" id="134529"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#134529" label="[13:45:29]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">homo: GHC 7.x stage 1 takes under 5 minutes on my system, and the later stages are actually built with the earlier stage</span></div><div class="line" id="134539"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#134539" label="[13:45:39]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">not with MicroHs</span></div><div class="line" id="134552"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#134552" label="[13:45:52]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's surprisingly fast</span></div><div class="line" id="134601"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#134601" label="[13:46:01]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">although the 1st stage will also run slow, since it's using the MicroHs runtime still</span></div><div class="line" id="134612"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#134612" label="[13:46:12]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">2nd stage is the one that's fully optimized</span></div><div class="line" id="134650"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#134650" label="[13:46:50]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">5 minutes is building GHC 7.6 stage 1 with 7.0</span></div><div class="line" id="134659"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#134659" label="[13:46:59]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">4.08.2 is probably faster</span></div><div class="line" id="134706"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#134706" label="[13:47:06]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">since it's smaller</span></div><div class="line" id="134725"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#134725" label="[13:47:25]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">4.08.2 has about 5x the Haskell code compared to mhs</span></div><div class="line" id="134758"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#134758" label="[13:47:58]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&amp; it builds twice (stage1 and stage2)</span></div><div class="line" id="134819"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#134819" label="[13:48:19]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so a GHC build using mhs is about 10x the time of rebuilding mhs with itself</span></div><div class="line" id="134830"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#134830" label="[13:48:30]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">That's _if_ it can be done, of course</span></div><div class="line" id="134908"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#134908" label="[13:49:08]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">mhs builds itself in like 20 seconds</span></div><div class="line" id="134920"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#134920" label="[13:49:20]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">5 minutes is 300 seconds</span></div><div class="line" id="134930"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#134930" label="[13:49:30]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">300 / 20 = 15</span></div><div class="line" id="135330"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#135330" label="[13:53:30]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">still, ghc doesn't support riscv, and there was no arm support in 4.08 and from what I heard is not great in recent ghc releases either</span></div><div class="line" id="154435"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#154435" label="[15:44:35]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">don't mean to interfere with the GHC conversation</span></div><div class="line" id="154451"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#154451" label="[15:44:51]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">since i kept focus on tinycc toolchain support for a _complete_ distribution</span></div><div class="line" id="154535"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#154535" label="[15:45:35]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">would Hasekell related parts that need a c-toolchain be supported with tinycc?</span></div><div class="line" id="154711"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#154711" label="[15:47:11]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if you can build hugs with tinycc, then you likely can build microhs with tinycc</span></div><div class="line" id="154743"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#154743" label="[15:47:43]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's the question homo, since i didn't try myself yet (tried to keep script interpreter dependencies at a minimum)</span></div><div class="line" id="154832"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#154832" label="[15:48:32]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the thing is using tinycc it's easier to build microhs than it is to build hugs</span></div><div class="line" id="154850"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#154850" label="[15:48:50]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also to build hugs you need yacc</span></div><div class="line" id="155406"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#155406" label="[15:54:06]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i remember haskell as a dependency of pandoc</span></div><div class="line" id="155529"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#155529" label="[15:55:29]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and decided to postpone that one since it didn't block any other task</span></div><div class="line" id="155600"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#155600" label="[15:56:00]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">currently, i could not retain ghostscript with tinycc neither</span></div><div class="line" id="155645"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#155645" label="[15:56:45]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ghostscript and pandoc aren't related, it's just two components relevant to scientific authoring and document format processing</span></div><div class="line" id="155844"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#155844" label="[15:58:44]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">mupdf didn't make it into the tinycc profile yet, since it conflicted with acceptance criteria</span></div><div class="line" id="160058"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#160058" label="[16:00:58]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i could not salvage a TeX implementation yet with tinycc/alternative-toolchain</span></div><div class="line" id="160137"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#160137" label="[16:01:37]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this is _not_ tinycc specific, it is just free software smashed against acceptance criteria of alternative toolchain support</span></div><div class="line" id="160722"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#160722" label="[16:07:22]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi I never heard anyone building pandoc without ghc, so if you want to try with microhs, you might need to modify source codes of pandoc and its dependencies, as well as modify source code of hugs to build under tinycc</span></div><div class="line" id="161037"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#161037" label="[16:10:37]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it shouldn't be a problem to modify hugs, as hugs can build even on something as weird as plan9</span></div><div class="line" id="173859"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#173859" label="[17:38:59]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Reminded me that 9front ported ghostscript but it's some ancient 8.53 release.</span></div><div class="line" id="173901"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#173901" label="[17:39:01]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="http://git.9front.org/plan9front/plan9front/63003069e3812b6f7a53996967919404635dfeef/sys/src/cmd/gs/mkfile/f.html">http://git.9front.org/plan9front/plan9front/63003069e3812b6f7a53996967919404635dfeef/sys/src/cmd/gs/mkfile/f.html</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="174121"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#174121" label="[17:41:21]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(checked proper file and yeah 8.53: <a rel="nofollow" href="http://git.9front.org/plan9front/plan9front/63003069e3812b6f7a53996967919404635dfeef/sys/src/cmd/gs/src/version.mak/f.html">http://git.9front.org/plan9front/plan9front/63003069e3812b6f7a53996967919404635dfeef/sys/src/cmd/gs/src/version.mak/f.html</a>  )</span></div><div class="line" id="181151"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#181151" label="[18:11:51]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lanodan alladin ghostscript is non-free only because it's illegal to sell it, but I bet it is actually free software to communists</span></div><div class="line" id="181655"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#181655" label="[18:16:55]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but if you don't mind that your distro is communist and thus is difficult to abuse by for-profit companies that don't want to pay salaries to workers, sure go ahead with alladin ghostscript</span></div><div class="line" id="201517"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#201517" label="[20:15:17]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; alladin ghostscript was just some fork</span></div><div class="line" id="201551"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#201551" label="[20:15:51]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; I think current version is under GNU AGPLv3</span></div><br /></div></body></html>